Refresh:
* Non-text screen modes:
 - 8 refreshes per line, 128 cycles in between.
 - 6 cycles per refresh.
 - Position in time in VDP cycles. I've indicated the falling edge of the CAS
   signal (because that was easiest to analyze using simple tools). This means
   that the full access starts one cycle earlier and the next access can only
   start 5 cycles later.
     285, 413, 541, 669, 797, 925, 1053, 1181
 - (As expected) this is independent of screen on/off or sprites on/off, or
   any other VDP activity.
* Text-mode:
 - There only are only 7 refreshes per display line
 - I didn't look into this further in more detail because it's of less
   interest for eventually figuring out the VDP command timings.
* Each refresh is a normal VRAM read (so the VDP doesn't seem to use the
  faster RAS-without-CAS refresh mechanism). On each refresh the high byte
  (bits 15-8) of the *logical* address increases by one. The low byte mostly
  remains constant at a value 0x3f, 0x7f, 0xbf or 0xff (bits 7-6 *seem* to
  increase every 64 refreshes, but I didn't look into this in detail, it
  also doesn't really matter for us). Bit 16 of the logical address toggles
  each refresh.


Display data (bitmap data):
* screen 5:
 - 32 blocks of 4 bytes + 1 preamble block
 - Each block reads 4 bytes in burst mode (takes 2+4*4 = 18 cycles)
 - The preamble block (comes before the 32 data blocks) reads 4 times
   address 0x1ffff.
 - There are 32 cycles between the start of each block, so that leaves
   14 cycles for other stuff. That is room for two accesses (2x6 = 12).
 - The VDS signal is set for the 32 data blocks, but not for the
   preamble block. (As we'll see later there are also no other accesses
   that set the VDS signal .. Though I only checked bitmap modes).
 - Positions (VDP clocks, falling edge of CAS):
    (195),  227,  259,  291,  323,  355,  387,  419,  451,
            483,  515,  547,  579,  611,  643,  675,  707,
            739,  771,  803,  835,  867,  899,  931,  963,
            995, 1027, 1059, 1091, 1123, 1155, 1187, 1219,
* screen 8:
 - Similar to screen 5. There are also 32 blocks plus one preamble. The
   start of these blocks happens at the same position (in VDP cycles) in the
   display line.
 - Each block now reads 8 bytes. These reads happen interleaved to the two
   memory banks (so alternating CAS0 and CAS1). So it's almost twice as fast:
   these 8 reads take 20 cycles.
 - This still leaves (32-20 = 12 = 2 * 6) room for two other access, just as
   in the screen 5 case.
 - The preamble block only reads one time address 0x1ffff (in screen 5 there
   were 4 reads from this address). Though (when display is enabled) there
   never seems to be another VRAM access in the 'time slot' of this preamble
   block. (And if you look at the VCD trace, the CAS1 signal remains high for
   the whole duration of this block. So it seems it's trying to do 8 reads
   from address 0x1ffff, but there's just no time to actually toggle the CAS1
   signal).

* screen 5+8:
 - Compared to screen 8, in screen 5 there seem to be 2 extra free cycles
   between the data blocks. Though in screen 5 the next VRAM access always
   starts two cycles after this block, so at the same relative moment in
   time compared to screen 8. Actually if you look at the VCD trace, also
   in screen 5 the RAS signal remains active for 2 cycles longer than strictly
   needed, so the RAS signal behaves the same in both modes. So from a
   VRAM time-slot allocation point of view both screen modes behave actually
   the same.
 - I wonder why there is such a dummy(?) preamble block. Theoretically
   this *could* have been used to implement V9958-like horizontal scroll
   without having to mask 8 border pixels. Unfortunately it doesn't work
   like that :(


Sprite mode 2
(I didn't look into sprite mode 1 yet, it's also of less interest for the
VDP command engine timings).

* Read 32 y-coordinates + 1 post-amble
 - The y-coord of each sprite in the attribute table (32 in total) is read
   byte per byte (so 6 x 32 cycles), interleaved with the reads of the bitmap
   blocks (see above).
 - At the end there's one dummy read of address 0x1ffff.
 - This is the same for 8x8 and 16x16 sprites.
 - Positions (VDP clock cycles):
    183,  215,  247,  279,  311,  343,  375,  407,
    439,  471,  503,  535,  567,  599,  631,  663,
    695,  727,  759,  791,  823,  855,  887,  919,
    951,  983, 1015, 1047, 1079, 1111, 1143, 1175, (1207)
 - There are always 32 reads, even if there are already 8 visible sprites
   (so the next reads are useless) or if there is a sprite with y-coord
   equal to 216.

* Read data to display the sprites
 - This happens in 4 chunks of each 2 sprites.
 - Even if there are less than 8 sprites to display, all read access still
   occurs. It _seems_ (I didn't look in detail) to be that the (useless)
   reads are duplicates of the sprite 0. (Or is it the first visible sprite?
   Anyway, not important for our purpose, it matters that the access slot
   remains occupied).
 - Each chunk reads:
    - y-coord, x-coord, pat-num of 1st sprite (burst of 3 reads, 13(!)cycles)
    - y-coord, x-coord, pat-num of 2nd sprite (burst of 3 reads, 13(!)cycles)
    - pause of 6 or 10(!) cycles
    - 2 pattern bytes of 1st sprite           (burst of 2 reads, 10 cycles)
    - color attribute of 1st sprite           (single read,       6 cycles)
    - 2 pattern bytes of 2nd sprite           (burst of 2 reads, 10 cycles)
    - color attribute of 2nd sprite           (single read,       6 cycles)
   Note that the 2 bursts of 3 reads take only 13 cycles instead of the
   expected 14 cycles. If you look at the VCD trace you also see that this
   uses an illegal(?) RAM access pattern: RAS is released before CAS is
   released. Though obviously this seems to work ... makes me wonder why they
   don't always use this faster pattern.
 - Even 8x8 sprites always fetch 2 bytes of pattern-data per sprite line
   (the 2nd byte is ignored).
 - Note that the y-coordinate is fetched again, it was already fetched to
   figure out which sprites are visible.
 - The positions in time are like this (VDP clock cycles):
    1239, 1243, 1247, 1252, 1256, 1260, *, 1271, 1275, 1281, 1287, 1291, 1297
    1303, 1307, 1311, 1316, 1320, 1324, #, 1339, 1343, 1349, 1355, 1359, 1365
       3,    7,   11,   16,   20,   24, *,   35,   39,   45,   51,   55,   61
      67,   71,   75,   80,   84,   88, *,   99,  103,  109,  115,  119,  125
  - Note that these time-numbers still use an arbitrary starting point, so
    the point where cycle numbers wrap from 1368->0 doesn't mean anything.
  - Also note that the timing is slightly irregular: in the 1st, 3rd and 4th
    group there's a pause of 6 cycles (there fits exactly one other access).
    But in ihe 2nd group there are 10 cycles in between. There also only fits
    one other access in this slot, and the timing is 2+6+2, so 2 'wasted'
    cycles before and after the other access.

* It's worth repeating that whenever sprites are enabled, the VDP _always_
  performs the same fetch-pattern. So even if no sprites are actually visible,
  or if sprites are partially disabled (with y=216), and even with 8x8 vs
  16x16 sprites. This confirms the fact that the VDP command engine is slowed
  down by the exact same amount in all these situation.
* Screen 5 and 8 have the exact same behavior wrt sprite data fetches.



Summary so far and slightly looking ahead:
* So far I've only looked at
   refresh, bitmap-fetches, sprite-fetches
  I didn't yet look at CPU-VRAM access or command-engine-VRAM-access. However
  I did already quickly check that the latter two accesses always use single-
  byte accesses (so 6 cycles).
* In the table below I've shown a timeline of one display line. I've indicated
  where each type of access occurs and where there are still empty slots for
  CPU or command access.
* Note that this table is *only* valid for the case screen enabled, sprites
  enabled.
* I don't know how horizontal set-adjust will influence this table, we also
  didn't do any measurements with varying set-adjust.

